# Bird-Eye Pin Anywhere

Three sub-features that turn the bird-eye overview into an interactive map:

1. **Zoom** — wheel / pinch adjusts the bird-eye camera's `focalLength` on the session layer.
2. **Click-anywhere pinning** — a tap on the stream places a pin snapped to the nearest magnet (waypoint or named spawn) in **XZ** when within `MAX_MAGNET_SNAP_XZ_CM`; otherwise to the nearest OSM road node, and opens the POI sheet.
3. **Directions** — the "Get directions" button hands the pin's world position to the **RouteComposer** (see `route-composer.mdc`), which computes a walkable polyline covering any combination of NavMesh and OSM legs. Play / Pause replay the composer's cached polyline.

All three phases reuse existing infrastructure: the POI sheet, the bird-eye directions panel, the `PointClickAutoMover`, the bird-eye route projector, the `OsmGraphService` (for the outside-click snap) and the `RouteComposer` (for everything else).

---

## Architecture overview

```
USER TAPS BIRD-EYE STREAM
       │  sendMessage('younite.pick.request', { intent: 'birdEyePin', ndcX, ndcY })
       ▼
younite.picking_extension — raycasts, emits younite.pick.result
       ▼
BirdEyePinService._on_pick_result              (navmesh_route_extension)
       │
       ├─ magnets.nearest_magnet_with_xz_dist2() — nearest nav_waypoint_* / PlayerSpawnPoint_*
       │
       ├─ XZ distance ≤ MAX_MAGNET_SNAP_XZ_CM
       │       → _emit_magnet_pin() → projector.set_pin_marker(pos)
       │       → dispatch 'birdEyePinSheet' (magnet pos + label + isInsideNavmesh:true)
       │
       └─ else → _handle_outside() → BirdEyePinOsmSnap.snap_click() (nearest OSM graph node)
                  → projector.set_pin_marker(snapped)
                  → dispatch 'birdEyePinSheet' (snapped pos + label + isInsideNavmesh:false)
       │
       ▼
MapMarkerPoiSheet — "Teleport" (inside pins only) / "Get directions"
       │
       └─ Get directions → forceActivatePinRoute({ id, title, worldPos })
                         → sendMessage('birdEyeRouteRequest', { endPos: worldPos })
       ▼
extension.py::_on_bird_eye_route_request
       │  composer.compose(player_world, end_world)
       │  → BirdEyeRouteProjector.on_route_computed(navmesh_points)
       │  → BirdEyeRouteProjector.set_osm_segment(osm_points)
       │  → dispatch 'navmeshRouteMeasure' (distance / ETA)
       ▼
MapMarkerDirectionsPanel — "Start navigation"
       │  sendMessage('mapMarkerDirectionsStart',
       │              { startPos, endPos, endUseStoredFp })
       ▼
feature_commands_service/map_marker_directions.py::MapMarkerDirectionsHandler._run
       │  composer.compose(start_world, end_world)   ← preview-only (yaw + fail-fast)
       │  async_first_person_enter(startSpawn, dispatch_ready=False)
       │  optional teleport_to_coordinates(start_pos, world_body_yaw_deg)  (swap case)
       │  dispatch navigationStateSet { routeId:'poi_nav', autoMove:false,
       │                                 endPos, useComposer:true, drawPath:true }
       │     └── orchestrator._apply_state → navmeshRouteCalculate
       │     └── NavMeshRouteService.set_route_for_id(use_composer=True)
       │     └── RouteInstance._compute_path → RouteComposer.compose(enable_corridor_routing=True)
       │     └── RouteEngine: waypoints + sphere trail + measure
       │  await await_navmesh_route_settled('poi_nav')
       │  dispatch viewTransitionReady(firstPerson)
       ▼
User presses Play / Pause
       │  Play : sendMessage('navigationStateSet',
       │          { routeId:'poi_nav', autoMove:true,
       │            endPos|endpointPath, useComposer:true })
       │  Pause: sendMessage('navigationStateSet',
       │          { routeId:'poi_nav', autoMove:false })
       │  → orchestrator._apply_state re-dispatches navmeshRouteCalculate
       │  → engine recomputes via composer (handles drift after free-walk)
       ▼
PointClickAutoMover — exact same path seat / POI / toilet use
       │  autoMove flip False→True reactivates from cached waypoints
```

The web side never knows whether a route is NavMesh-only, OSM-bridged, or cross-island — that's the composer's job. Web only ships start / end world positions.

