## Phase 2 — Click-anywhere pin with magnet snap

### Pick intent

`AppStream.tsx` adds a `birdEyePin` branch to its pick-request pipeline **before** the default `!isBirdEye` guard so bird-eye pick results route to the pin service rather than to teleport / interaction.

The branch is **suppressed once a route polyline is already drawn** on the bird-eye overlay (`birdEyeRouteActive`) — the directions panel is already locked to its target, and re-pinning would silently mutate the destination with no way for the user to confirm it.

### Magnet targets (in-arena snap)

| Magnet | USD prim | Label source |
|---|---|---|
| Corridor waypoint | `nav_waypoint_*` (from `waypoint_router._scan_waypoints`) | `labels.waypoint_label()` — entrance suffix → "Entrance N", single-letter → "Section X", two-letter → "Near X / Y" |
| Named spawn point | `/World/PlayerSpawnPoint_<Name>` (digit-suffixed are camera spawns, skipped) | `constants.SPAWN_POINT_LABELS`, else humanized suffix |

`magnets.nearest_magnet_with_xz_dist2()` returns the nearest magnet by **horizontal** distance from the 3D hit (roof clicks use the same plan-view column as ground). If that squared XZ distance is ≤ `MAX_MAGNET_SNAP_XZ_CM`², the pin lands **on** the magnet; otherwise the click is treated as outside (OSM snap). Adding waypoints shrinks dead zones inside that radius without navmesh queries.

### Outside (no nearby magnet) snap

`BirdEyePinOsmSnap.snap_click()` (`bird_eye_pin_service/osm.py`) runs `OsmGraphService.nearest_node(click - roadsOffset, mode=<active OSM mode>, component=main_component)` and re-applies the offset. The pin lands on the closest **mode-reachable** OSM graph node (using the node's authored Y so the flag sits on the road plane, not on a roof / lawn / waterway picked by the raycast).

Mode resolution reads the production CU accessibility state from `NavMeshModeCache.get_active_mode()` and maps it onto the OSM mode (`walking` / `wheelchair`); the component id is resolved against the same mode-scoped labelling, so in wheelchair mode the pin cannot snap to a foot-only node that is unreachable from the main wheelchair-connected subgraph.

No OSM route is planned at pin time — route composition is entirely deferred to the `RouteComposer` when the user presses "Get directions". The composer resolves the same mode via its own `_active_osm_mode()` helper, so the snap and the composed route always agree on reachability.

### Sheet payload (`birdEyePinSheet`)

| Field | Purpose |
|---|---|
| `id` | Synthetic `pin_{int(x)}_{int(z)}` stable key |
| `title` | Magnet label (inside) or "Pinned location" (outside / no magnet) |
| `worldPos` | Where the pin flag renders; used as the composer end-point on "Get directions" |
| `isInsideNavmesh` | UI gate for the teleport button only (outside pins would land in dead geometry) |
| `magnetName` | Debug / future affordance |
| `spawnPoint` | Inside pin that snapped to a `PlayerSpawnPoint_*` — enables the regular POI-sheet teleport path |

### UI branches (`MapMarkerPoiSheet`)

- **Teleport** — enabled for inside pins (dispatches `pinTeleport` with world coords) or regular POIs (existing `teleportToSpawnpoint`). Outside pins cannot teleport.
- **Get directions** — enabled for any pin with a `worldPos`. Always dispatches `birdEyeRouteRequest { endPos: worldPos }`; the RouteComposer figures out the rest.

### Deferred route draw (pin sheet = markers only)

The pin sheet is purely informational — it does not pre-commit route geometry:

- On pin placement Kit pushes only the **solo pin marker** via `projector.set_pin_marker(world)`.
- `BirdEyeRouteOverlay` renders the pin marker as a standalone flag bubble when no polyline is active (`layout.pinOnly`). The user-location arrow continues to show via the existing `userLocation` projectable.
- "Get directions" dispatches `birdEyeRouteRequest`; the Kit handler calls `clear_pin_marker()` (the route's destination bubble replaces the flag), then the composer produces a polyline fed via `on_route_computed` + `set_osm_segment`.
- Dismissing without committing (X, back, teleport) fires `birdEyePinDismiss` which clears both the pin marker and any stale OSM segment. The dismiss is gated by a `transitioningToDirectionsRef` in the sheet so "Get directions" does not race with its own `birdEyeRouteRequest`.

